3. [Maximum mark: 13]
(a) Let $V$ be the set of three-dimensional vectors. A relation $R$ is defined on $V$ by $\boldsymbol{a}R\boldsymbol{b}$ if and only if $\boldsymbol{a} \cdot \boldsymbol{b} = 0$. Determine with reasons whether $R$ is
(i) reflexive;
(ii) symmetric;
(iii) transitive. [3]
(b) Let $W$ be the set of non-zero three-dimensional vectors. A relation $S$ is defined on $W$ by $\boldsymbol{a}S\boldsymbol{b}$ if and only if $\boldsymbol{a} \times \boldsymbol{b} = \boldsymbol{0}$. Determine with reasons whether $S$ is
(i) reflexive;
(ii) symmetric;
(iii) transitive. [5]
(c) (i) Exactly one of $R$ and $S$ is an equivalence relation. State which relation this is.
(ii) For this equivalence relation, $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ y \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}$ belongs to the equivalence class containing $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$. Find the value of $y$. [3]
(d) The relation $S$ from part (b) is now defined on the set $V$ from part (a). Determine, with a reason, whether $S$ is transitive on $V$. [2]
